text:
VAR (Vector AutoRegressive) is a multivariate1 linear time series model, designed to capture the joint dynamics2 of multiple time series through an interconnected system.
VARs, by default, treat each endogenous variable3 in the system as a function of lagged values of all endogenous variables. Exogenous variables enter only when explicitly added.
VARs offer a simple4 and flexible alternative to traditional multiple equation models5.


3.1.1 A closer look: joint dynamics, interconnected system and endogeneity
In a VAR the variables are not studied side by side. They form a single system, where each one drives the others and is driven by them. This happens because each variable appears on the left-hand side of its own equation and on the right-hand side of the others. Since every variable has its own equation, each one is explained within the system, and none can be treated as exogenous: this is exactly what endogeneity means here.
The link is not a one-off cross-effect, nor does it run in one direction only: it is mutual, and it propagates forward indefinitely.
Example:
To fix ideas, let inflation (\(\pi\)) and GDP each depend on both their own lag and the other’s lag. Since the true data-generating process is unknown, the relationship is left as a generic function:
\[\begin{aligned}
\pi_t &= f(\pi_{t-1},\, gdp_{t-1}) \\
gdp_t &= f(gdp_{t-1},\, \pi_{t-1})
\end{aligned}\]
Following a single cross-channel makes the mechanism visible and more intuitive. Take the \(gdp \to \pi\) link:
\[gdp_{t-1} \;\rightarrow\; \pi_t \;\rightarrow\; gdp_{t+1} \;\rightarrow\; \pi_{t+2} \;\rightarrow\; gdp_{t+3} \;\rightarrow\; \cdots\]
This single thread is real, but on its own it understates the structure. Own lags run alongside these cross-links, and each own lag is itself shaped by the other variable one period earlier: \(gdp_{t-1}\) already carries \(\pi_{t-2}\). Every value is therefore fed, at every period, by both variables at once; as shown in the animation below, where horizontal arrows are own lags (persistence) and diagonal arrows are cross-lags (spillovers).
This self-sustaining feedback loop is what makes the system genuinely joint, and it is the aspect most often underestimated when first learning VAR.

{
  const GDP = 0, PI = 1;
  const periods = ["t\u22121", "t", "t+1", "t+2"];
  const N = periods.length;

  const colGDP = "#4c78a8", colPI = "#e45756";
  const faint = "#ececec", firedCol = "#f6c667", hotArrow = "#f0a202";
  const coldFill = "#f5f5f5", coldStroke = "#cfcfcf";
  const colOf = r =&gt; (r === GDP ? colGDP : colPI);

  const width = 680, height = 300;
  const mL = 88, mR = 30;
  const dx = (width - mL - mR) / (N - 1);
  const legendY = 22, periodY = 58, yTop = 104, yBot = 224, captionY = 288;
  const R = 15;
  const x = i =&gt; mL + i * dx;
  const yOf = r =&gt; (r === GDP ? yTop : yBot);

  const frames = [
    {type:"start", caption:"Start at t\u22121: GDP and \u03C0 are both given."},
    {type:"trans", col:0, caption:"Both feed forward: own lags (horizontal) and cross-lags (diagonal) fire together."},
    {type:"trans", col:1, caption:"The same pattern repeats: t feeds t+1 through all four channels."},
    {type:"trans", col:2, caption:"\u2026and again, the feedback loop sustains itself, forward without end."},
    {type:"hold",  caption:"Every value is fed by both variables: one joint, interconnected system."},
    {type:"hold",  caption:"Every value is fed by both variables: one joint, interconnected system."}
  ];
  const total = frames.length;

  function stateAt(f) {
    const hot = [Array(N).fill(false), Array(N).fill(false)];
    hot[GDP][0] = true;
    hot[PI][0]  = true;
    const fired = new Set();
    let current = [];
    for (let idx = 1; idx &lt;= f && idx &lt; total; idx++) {
      const fr = frames[idx];
      if (fr.type !== "trans") continue;
      const c = fr.col;
      const arrowsThis = [];
      for (let r = 0; r &lt; 2; r++) {
        if (hot[r][c]) {
          arrowsThis.push([c, r, c + 1, GDP]);
          arrowsThis.push([c, r, c + 1, PI]);
        }
      }
      hot[GDP][c + 1] = true; hot[PI][c + 1] = true;
      if (idx === f) current = arrowsThis;
      else arrowsThis.forEach(a =&gt; fired.add(a.join(",")));
    }
    return {hot, fired, current, caption: frames[f].caption};
  }

  const container = d3.create("div").style("font-family", "system-ui, sans-serif");
  const controls = container.append("div")
    .style("display", "flex").style("gap", "6px").style("align-items", "center")
    .style("margin-bottom", "6px");
  const mkBtn = (label) =&gt; controls.append("button").text(label)
    .style("padding", "3px 10px").style("font-size", "13px")
    .style("border", "1px solid #ccc").style("border-radius", "6px")
    .style("background", "#fff").style("cursor", "pointer");
  const playBtn = mkBtn("Pause");
  const resetBtn = mkBtn("Reset");
  const prevBtn = mkBtn("\u2039");
  const nextBtn = mkBtn("\u203A");
  const holder = container.append("div");

  let f = 0, playing = true;

  function drawArrow(svg, i0, r0, i1, r1, mode) {
    const x0 = x(i0), y0 = yOf(r0), x1 = x(i1), y1 = yOf(r1);
    const a = Math.atan2(y1 - y0, x1 - x0);
    const sx = x0 + R * Math.cos(a), sy = y0 + R * Math.sin(a);
    const ex = x1 - (R + 5) * Math.cos(a), ey = y1 - (R + 5) * Math.sin(a);
    const col = mode === "hot" ? hotArrow : mode === "fired" ? firedCol : faint;
    const w = mode === "hot" ? 3.5 : mode === "fired" ? 2.5 : 1.2;
    svg.append("line")
      .attr("x1", sx).attr("y1", sy).attr("x2", ex).attr("y2", ey)
      .attr("stroke", col).attr("stroke-width", w)
      .attr("opacity", mode === "faint" ? 0.6 : 1)
      .attr("marker-end", "url(#ah-" + mode + ")");
  }

  function update() {
    const st = stateAt(f);
    holder.selectAll("*").remove();
    const svg = holder.append("svg")
      .attr("viewBox", [0, 0, width, height])
      .attr("width", width)
      .attr("style", "max-width:100%;height:auto;");

    const defs = svg.append("defs");
    [["faint", faint], ["fired", firedCol], ["hot", hotArrow]].forEach(([id, c]) =&gt; {
      defs.append("marker").attr("id", "ah-" + id)
        .attr("viewBox", "0 0 10 10").attr("refX", 9).attr("refY", 5)
        .attr("markerWidth", 7).attr("markerHeight", 7).attr("orient", "auto")
        .append("path").attr("d", "M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 z").attr("fill", c);
    });

    // row baselines + labels (pi bigger)
    [["GDP", GDP, 16], ["\u03C0", PI, 26]].forEach(([lab, r, fs]) =&gt; {
      svg.append("line").attr("x1", mL - 20).attr("x2", width - mR)
        .attr("y1", yOf(r)).attr("y2", yOf(r)).attr("stroke", "#f2f2f2");
      svg.append("text").attr("x", mL - 34).attr("y", yOf(r) + fs / 3)
        .attr("text-anchor", "end").attr("font-weight", "bold")
        .attr("font-size", fs).attr("fill", colOf(r)).text(lab);
    });

    periods.forEach((p, i) =&gt; {
      svg.append("text").attr("x", x(i)).attr("y", periodY)
        .attr("text-anchor", "middle").attr("font-size", 12).attr("fill", "#999").text(p);
    });

    // faint full lattice
    for (let i = 0; i &lt; N - 1; i++) {
      drawArrow(svg, i, GDP, i + 1, GDP, "faint");
      drawArrow(svg, i, PI,  i + 1, PI,  "faint");
      drawArrow(svg, i, GDP, i + 1, PI,  "faint");
      drawArrow(svg, i, PI,  i + 1, GDP, "faint");
    }
    // already-fired
    st.fired.forEach(k =&gt; {
      const a = k.split(",").map(Number);
      drawArrow(svg, a[0], a[1], a[2], a[3], "fired");
    });
    // current transition: all arrows together
    st.current.forEach(a =&gt; drawArrow(svg, a[0], a[1], a[2], a[3], "hot"));

    // nodes
    for (let r = 0; r &lt; 2; r++) {
      for (let i = 0; i &lt; N; i++) {
        const isHot = st.hot[r][i];
        svg.append("circle").attr("cx", x(i)).attr("cy", yOf(r)).attr("r", R)
          .attr("fill", isHot ? colOf(r) : coldFill)
          .attr("stroke", isHot ? colOf(r) : coldStroke).attr("stroke-width", 2);
      }
    }

    // gentle highlight of both starting values at t-1 (no shock language)
    if (frames[f].type === "start") {
      [GDP, PI].forEach(r =&gt; {
        svg.append("circle").attr("cx", x(0)).attr("cy", yOf(r)).attr("r", R + 5)
          .attr("fill", "none").attr("stroke", colOf(r)).attr("stroke-width", 2)
          .attr("opacity", 0.55);
      });
    }

    // legend (raised)
    const lx = width - mR - 250, ly = legendY;
    svg.append("line").attr("x1", lx).attr("y1", ly).attr("x2", lx + 24).attr("y2", ly)
      .attr("stroke", "#bbb").attr("stroke-width", 2).attr("marker-end", "url(#ah-faint)");
    svg.append("text").attr("x", lx + 30).attr("y", ly + 4).attr("font-size", 11).attr("fill", "#666").text("own lag");
    svg.append("line").attr("x1", lx + 92).attr("y1", ly - 6).attr("x2", lx + 116).attr("y2", ly + 6)
      .attr("stroke", "#bbb").attr("stroke-width", 2).attr("marker-end", "url(#ah-faint)");
    svg.append("text").attr("x", lx + 122).attr("y", ly + 4).attr("font-size", 11).attr("fill", "#666").text("cross-lag");

    // caption + counter
    svg.append("text").attr("x", width / 2).attr("y", captionY)
      .attr("text-anchor", "middle").attr("font-size", 13).attr("fill", "#333").text(st.caption);
    svg.append("text").attr("x", width - mR).attr("y", captionY)
      .attr("text-anchor", "end").attr("font-size", 11).attr("fill", "#bbb")
      .text((f + 1) + "/" + total);
  }

  playBtn.on("click", () =&gt; { playing = !playing; playBtn.text(playing ? "Pause" : "Play"); });
  resetBtn.on("click", () =&gt; { f = 0; update(); });
  prevBtn.on("click", () =&gt; { playing = false; playBtn.text("Play"); f = (f - 1 + total) % total; update(); });
  nextBtn.on("click", () =&gt; { playing = false; playBtn.text("Play"); f = (f + 1) % total; update(); });

  const timer = setInterval(() =&gt; { if (playing) { f = (f + 1) % total; update(); } }, 1300);
  invalidation.then(() =&gt; clearInterval(timer));

  update();
  return container.node();
}

3.2 VAR from a mathematical POV
A VAR is an \(n\)-equation, \(n\)-variable linear model in which each variable is explained by its own lagged values, plus current (initially this term will missed, it appears later in the text) and past values of the remaining \(n-1\) variables.
Formally, let \(Z_t\) be a vector collecting the values of \(n\) variables at time \(t\), for instance inflation, output gap, and the interest rate. A vector autoregressive process of order \(p\), written VAR(\(p\)), generalizes the one-variable AR(\(p\)) process to \(n\) variables:
\[Z_t = c + \Phi_1 Z_{t-1} + \Phi_2 Z_{t-2} + \cdots + \Phi_p Z_{t-p} + w_t
\tag{3.1}\]
where:
\[Z_t = \begin{bmatrix} z_{1t} \\ z_{2t} \\ \vdots \\ z_{nt} \end{bmatrix}, \qquad
c = \begin{bmatrix} c_1 \\ c_2 \\ \vdots \\ c_n \end{bmatrix}, \qquad
\Phi_j = \begin{bmatrix} \phi_{11}^{(j)} & \cdots & \phi_{1n}^{(j)} \\ \vdots & \ddots & \vdots \\ \phi_{n1}^{(j)} & \cdots & \phi_{nn}^{(j)} \end{bmatrix}, \qquad
w_t = \begin{bmatrix} w_{1t} \\ w_{2t} \\ \vdots \\ w_{nt} \end{bmatrix}
\tag{3.2}\]
The model describes the evolution of \(Z_t\) as a function of its own past realizations \(Z_{t-1}, \dots, Z_{t-p}\) and of a vector of white noise errors \(w_t\).






NoteDeeper dive: How the system grows with n variable and p lags





The compact form of Equation 3.1 hides how quickly the system grows once \(n\) and \(p\) increase. The interactive below writes out the full set of equations for any chosen number of variables and lags (max 4 and 4), and tracks the number of parameters this entails.

{
  const katex = await require("katex@0.16.9");
  if (!document.querySelector("link[data-katex-css]")) {
    const link = document.createElement("link");
    link.rel = "stylesheet";
    link.href = "https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/KaTeX/0.16.9/katex.min.css";
    link.setAttribute("data-katex-css", "1");
    document.head.appendChild(link);
  }

  const maxN = 4, maxP = 4;
  const cCoef = "#2f6fb0", cConst = "#777", cShock = "#c0392b";

  const container = d3.create("div").style("font-family", "system-ui, sans-serif");

  const controls = container.append("div")
    .style("display", "flex").style("gap", "28px").style("align-items", "center")
    .style("flex-wrap", "wrap").style("margin-bottom", "18px");

  function stepper(label, min, max, initial) {
    const wrap = controls.append("div").style("display", "flex")
      .style("align-items", "center").style("gap", "10px");
    wrap.append("span").style("font-size", "13px").style("color", "#333")
      .style("font-weight", "600").text(label);
    const group = wrap.append("div").style("display", "flex").style("align-items", "center")
      .style("border", "1px solid #cfcfcf").style("border-radius", "8px")
      .style("overflow", "hidden").style("background", "#fff")
      .style("box-shadow", "inset 0 1px 2px rgba(0,0,0,0.04)");
    const mk = (txt) =&gt; group.append("button").html(txt)
      .style("width", "30px").style("height", "30px").style("border", "none")
      .style("background", "#f7f7f7").style("cursor", "pointer")
      .style("font-size", "16px").style("line-height", "1").style("color", "#444")
      .style("display", "flex").style("align-items", "center").style("justify-content", "center");
    const minus = mk("\u2212");
    minus.style("border-right", "1px solid #e2e2e2");
    const val = group.append("span").style("min-width", "34px").style("text-align", "center")
      .style("font-size", "15px").style("font-weight", "700").style("color", "#222").text(initial);
    const plus = mk("+");
    plus.style("border-left", "1px solid #e2e2e2");
    let v = initial;
    const set = nv =&gt; { v = Math.max(min, Math.min(max, nv)); val.text(v); render(); };
    minus.on("click", () =&gt; set(v - 1));
    plus.on("click", () =&gt; set(v + 1));
    return { get: () =&gt; v };
  }

  const nCtrl = stepper("Variables (n)", 1, maxN, 2);
  const pCtrl = stepper("Lags (p)", 1, maxP, 1);

  const holder = container.append("div");

  function render() {
    const n = nCtrl.get(), p = pCtrl.get();
    holder.selectAll("*").remove();

    const rows = [];
    for (let i = 1; i &lt;= n; i++) {
      let r = "\\mathbf{z_{" + i + ",t}} &= \\textcolor{" + cConst + "}{c_{" + i + "}}";
      for (let lag = 1; lag &lt;= p; lag++) {
        for (let k = 1; k &lt;= n; k++) {
          const sup = p &gt; 1 ? "^{(" + lag + ")}" : "";
          r += " + \\textcolor{" + cCoef + "}{\\phi_{" + i + k + "}" + sup + "}\\, z_{" + k + ",t-" + lag + "}";
        }
      }
      r += " + \\textcolor{" + cShock + "}{w_{" + i + ",t}}";
      rows.push(r);
    }
    const latex = "\\begin{aligned}" + rows.join(" \\\\[4pt] ") + "\\end{aligned}";

    const eqBox = holder.append("div")
      .style("background", "#fcfcfb").style("border", "1px solid #ececec")
      .style("border-radius", "10px").style("padding", "20px 22px")
      .style("overflow-x", "auto");
    eqBox.node().innerHTML = katex.renderToString(latex, { throwOnError: false, displayMode: true });

    const arCoef = p * n * n;
    const totalCoef = n + arCoef;
    const omega = n * (n + 1) / 2;

    const stat = holder.append("div")
      .style("margin-top", "16px").style("display", "flex").style("gap", "10px")
      .style("flex-wrap", "wrap");

    const card = (big, small, hot) =&gt; {
      const c = stat.append("div")
        .style("flex", "1").style("min-width", "150px")
        .style("background", hot ? "#fff7e6" : "#f5f7fa")
        .style("border", "1px solid " + (hot ? "#f0c667" : "#e2e8f0"))
        .style("border-radius", "8px").style("padding", "10px 12px");
      c.append("div").style("font-size", "22px").style("font-weight", "bold")
        .style("color", hot ? "#b8770a" : "#333").text(big);
      c.append("div").style("font-size", "11px").style("color", "#777").html(small);
    };

    card(arCoef, "AR coefficients&lt;br&gt;p\u00B7n\u00B2 = " + p + "\u00B7" + n + "\u00B2", true);
    card(totalCoef, "total coefficients&lt;br&gt;n + p\u00B7n\u00B2", false);
    card(omega, "\u03A9 parameters&lt;br&gt;n(n+1)/2", false);
  }

  render();
  return container.node();
}






The parameter counts shown in the three box follow a simple rule. Each of the \(n\) equations carries a constant plus \(n\) coefficients per lag, plus the autoregressive part that grows as \(pn^2\): linearly in the lags, but quadratically in the variables, giving \(n + pn^2\) across the system.
The covariance matrix \(\Omega\) adds a further \(n(n+1)/2\) parameters. So a modest VAR with \(n = 4\) and \(p = 4\) already needs \(5 + 4\cdot 16 = 69\) coefficients, estimated from macroeconomic samples that are rarely long.
This is the curse of dimensionality of VARs, and the reason parsimony in \(n\) and \(p\) is central to specification.




3.2.1 The error structure
The white-noise assumption on \(w_t\) is central and deserves to be stated precisely. Each error has zero mean and finite variance, is serially uncorrelated within equation over time but it may be correlated across equations at the same time. This distinction is crucial and recurs multiple time throughout the text.
Formally:
\[\operatorname{E}[w_t] = 0, \qquad
\operatorname{E}[w_t w_\tau'] =
\begin{cases}
\Omega & \text{if } t = \tau \\
0 & \text{if } t \neq \tau
\end{cases}
\tag{3.3}\]
More precisely:

Along the time dimension, the errors are uncorrelated with their own past and with all the right-hand-side variables, the lagged \(Z\)’s. This absence of serial correlation is what makes OLS, applied equation by equation, consistent.
Across equations, by contrast, \(\Omega\) is not assumed to be diagonal6, so the errors of different equations can be correlated at the same date \(t\). This non-zero off-diagonal is the trace left by the contemporaneous links between the variables: how in reality they influence each other in \(t\). This is the reason why, starting to be precise with name and classification, Equation 3.1 is called reduced-form VAR: the contemporaneous variables, the ones quoted earlier, are apparently absent from the right-hand side.

This cross-equation correlation is not a nuisance to be removed but the core point of the reduced form. The general picture can now begin to take shape. Contemporaneous variables are kept off the right-hand side because their presence would make the model impossible to estimate, as shown below; what is estimated is therefore the so-called reduced form.
Yet the trace of how the variables influence one another within period \(t\) does not vanish: it hides in the off-diagonal entries of the variance-covariance matrix, which record the covariances between the errors of the different equations at the same date. That single object, \(\Omega\), is where the whole contemporaneous structure now lives, and it deserves examination on its own.

3.2.1.1 The error variance covariance matrix
The matrix \(\Omega\) introduced in Equation 3.3 is the contemporaneous variance-covariance matrix of the error vector \(w_t\). In a reduced-form VAR it is the only place where the contemporaneous relations among the variables survive, and for this reason it carries the whole weight that identification will later have to lift.
Its content follows directly from the definition \(\Omega = \operatorname{E}[w_t w_t']\). Taking \(n = 2\), the outer product of the error vector with itself is
\[w_t w_t' = \begin{bmatrix} w_{1t} \\ w_{2t} \end{bmatrix} \begin{bmatrix} w_{1t} & w_{2t} \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} w_{1t}^2 & w_{1t} w_{2t} \\ w_{2t} w_{1t} & w_{2t}^2 \end{bmatrix},\]
so taking expectations entry by entry gives
\[\Omega = \begin{bmatrix} \operatorname{E}[w_{1t}^2] & \operatorname{E}[w_{1t} w_{2t}] \\ \operatorname{E}[w_{2t} w_{1t}] & \operatorname{E}[w_{2t}^2] \end{bmatrix}.\]
The white-noise assumption fixes what each entry is. Because the errors have zero mean, the general definitions of variance and covariance simplify:
\[\operatorname{Var}(w_{it}) = \operatorname{E}[w_{it}^2] - \underbrace{(\operatorname{E}[w_{it}])^2}_{=\,0} = \operatorname{E}[w_{it}^2], \qquad
\operatorname{Cov}(w_{it}, w_{jt}) = \operatorname{E}[w_{it} w_{jt}] - \underbrace{\operatorname{E}[w_{it}]\operatorname{E}[w_{jt}]}_{=\,0} = \operatorname{E}[w_{it} w_{jt}].\]
The diagonal entries \(\operatorname{E}[w_{it}^2]\) are therefore the variances \(\sigma_i^2\), and the off-diagonal entries \(\operatorname{E}[w_{it} w_{jt}]\) the contemporaneous covariances \(\sigma_{ij}\).
Written out, for clarification, for \(n = 2\) and \(n = 3\) the matrix takes the following form:
\[\Omega_{(n=2)} = \begin{bmatrix} \sigma_1^2 & \sigma_{12} \\ \sigma_{12} & \sigma_2^2 \end{bmatrix},
\qquad
\Omega_{(n=3)} = \begin{bmatrix} \sigma_1^2 & \sigma_{12} & \sigma_{13} \\ \sigma_{12} & \sigma_2^2 & \sigma_{23} \\ \sigma_{13} & \sigma_{23} & \sigma_3^2 \end{bmatrix}\] {#eq-omega-examples}
Two things can be read from this structure directly. As said before the diagonal collects the variances \(\sigma_i^2\), one per equation, measuring how volatile each equation’s own shock is; instead the off-diagonal collects the contemporaneous covariances \(\sigma_{ij}\), measuring how strongly the shocks of equations \(i\) and \(j\) move together at the same date. The matrix is symmetric, since \(\sigma_{ij} = \sigma_{ji}\), so the co-movement between two equations is a single number, not two. A diagonal \(\Omega\), with every \(\sigma_{ij} = 0\), would describe a system whose shocks are mutually uncorrelated, each equation disturbed on its own; in macroeconomic data this is the exception rather than the rule.
A non-zero off-diagonal is thus the footprint of the contemporaneous links among the variables, now stored in \(\Omega\) rather than written explicitly on the right-hand side. So far no restriction has been imposed: the residuals are not orthogonal7, and they cannot yet be read as structural shocks. Disentangling them is the task of identification, taken up in Chapter 5.
The easiest case as example:
The easiest case is a VAR(1), with just two variables \(x_t\) and \(y_t\).
It could be expressed in equation-by-equation form:
\[\begin{aligned}
x_t &= c_1 + \phi_{11}\, x_{t-1} + \phi_{12}\, y_{t-1} + w_{1t} \\
y_t &= c_2 + \phi_{21}\, x_{t-1} + \phi_{22}\, y_{t-1} + w_{2t}
\end{aligned}\] {#eq-var1-equations}
Or in matrix form:
\[Z_t = c + \Phi\, Z_{t-1} + w_t\] {#eq-var1-matrix-const}
Where:
\[Z_t = \begin{bmatrix} x_t \\ y_t \end{bmatrix}, \qquad
\Phi = \begin{bmatrix} \phi_{11} & \phi_{12} \\ \phi_{21} & \phi_{22} \end{bmatrix}, \qquad
w_t = \begin{bmatrix} w_{1t} \\ w_{2t} \end{bmatrix}\] {#eq-var1-matrices}
It is easy to verify that ?eq-var1-matrix-const is equivalent to ?eq-var1-equations by expanding the matrix product:
\[\Phi Z_{t-1} = \begin{bmatrix} \phi_{11} & \phi_{12} \\ \phi_{21} & \phi_{22} \end{bmatrix} \begin{bmatrix} x_{t-1} \\ y_{t-1} \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} \phi_{11} x_{t-1} + \phi_{12} y_{t-1} \\ \phi_{21} x_{t-1} + \phi_{22} y_{t-1} \end{bmatrix}\] {#eq-var1-expansion}

3.3 Why VARs are simple, flexible, and widely used
A standard AR or ARDL model8 is inherently unidirectional: the researcher must decide, a priori, which variable belongs on the left-hand side and which on the right. The true data-generating process (DGP), however, is always unobservable. There is no way to verify whether the imposed relationship reflects reality or is merely a convenient assumption.
VARs sidestep this problem by dropping the constraint of unidirectionality. As shown above, every variable can affect, and be affected by, every other, with no need to decide in advance who is endogenous and who is exogenous.
In microeconomics this chicken-and-egg problem is usually resolved through a natural temporal ordering: education precedes wages, so treating it as exogenous is defensible. In macroeconomics such precedence rarely exists: to continue the earlier example, GDP and inflation are determined simultaneously, within the same period.
This is what makes the VAR both simpler and more flexible: it is essentially atheoretical in structure, treating every variable as potentially endogenous and letting the data reveal the dynamics, rather than imposing a priori who (exogenous) influences whom (endogenous).

3.3.1 A closer look: simultaneity and the reduced form
The claim that GDP and inflation are determined “within the same period” deserves a closer look, because it is the reason a VAR is estimated in the particular form it is. This section gives the intuition and the essential steps; the full treatment, including how the underlying shocks are recovered, is the subject of Chapter 5.
Simultaneity means that two variables are set together, in the same instant, each responding to the other within the period. This differs from the lagged feedback of the animation above, where GDP at \(t-1\) moved inflation at \(t\): there the influence travelled through time, from one period to the next, and posed no difficulty for estimation. Simultaneity is sharper. Output lifts prices and inflation lifts output (the erosion of purchasing power drives consumption today to avoid paying higher prices tomorrow) in the same quarter , so neither can be placed first on the right-hand side. This is the chicken-and-egg problem in its strict form in macroeconometric.
The rest of this section unpacks the problem in four steps: writing the honest model, seeing why it cannot be estimated, finding the way out, and tracking where the simultaneity ends up.

The true and honest model:


If \(x\) and \(y\) are truly determined together, the faithful description places each contemporaneous variable on the other’s right-hand side:
\[\begin{aligned}
x_t &= \beta_{12}\, y_t + \gamma_{11}\, x_{t-1} + \gamma_{12}\, y_{t-1} + \eta_{1t}\\
y_t &= \beta_{21}\, x_t + \gamma_{21}\, x_{t-1} + \gamma_{22}\, y_{t-1} + \eta_{2t}
\end{aligned}\] {#eq-svar-structural}
The shocks \(\eta_{1t}, \eta_{2t}\) are the structural shocks, also called primitive impulses, structural innovations, or fundamental shocks in the literature. They are the genuine economic driving forces, a monetary-policy shock or a technology shock for instance, and they are uncorrelated with each other.
Moving the contemporaneous terms \(\beta_{12}\, y_t\) and \(\beta_{21}\, x_t\) to the left-hand side gathers everything dated \(t\) on one side. This regrouping is what the matrix \(B\) encodes, and it is done for computational convenience: with the period-\(t\) variables collected together, the system can later be solved in one step by premultiplying by \(B^{-1}\). In matrix form it becomes \(B\, Z_t = \Gamma\, Z_{t-1} + \eta_t\), with
\[\begin{aligned}
&B = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & -\beta_{12} \\ -\beta_{21} & 1 \end{bmatrix}, \qquad
\Gamma = \begin{bmatrix} \gamma_{11} & \gamma_{12} \\ \gamma_{21} & \gamma_{22} \end{bmatrix}, \qquad
\eta_t = \begin{bmatrix} \eta_{1t} \\ \eta_{2t} \end{bmatrix} \\[8pt]
&Z_t = \begin{bmatrix} x_t \\ y_t \end{bmatrix}, \qquad
Z_{t-1} = \begin{bmatrix} x_{t-1} \\ y_{t-1} \end{bmatrix}
\end{aligned}\] {#eq-svar-B}
Each piece plays a distinct role. \(Z_t\) stacks the two contemporaneous variables, and \(Z_{t-1}\) their first lags. On the left, \(B\) holds the contemporaneous links: ones on the diagonal, so each variable enters its own equation with coefficient one, and the off-diagonal entries \(-\beta_{12}, -\beta_{21}\) carrying the simultaneous effects moved over from the right. On the right, \(\Gamma\) holds the lag coefficients, mapping how yesterday’s values \(Z_{t-1}\) feed today’s, and \(\eta_t\) collects the two structural shocks. If the off-diagonal entries of \(B\) were zero, \(B\) would reduce to the identity and there would be no simultaneity left to deal with.

Why it cannot be estimated:


OLS delivers a reliable and consistent estimate under one essential condition: each regressor must be uncorrelated with the error of its own equation9. Consider estimating the first equation of ?eq-svar-structural and apply that single test. The contemporaneous regressor is \(y_t\) and the error is \(\eta_{1t}\). Trace the chain: \(y_t\) is set by the second equation, where it depends on \(x_t\); and \(x_t\) is set by the first equation, where it carries \(\eta_{1t}\). Putting these together, \(\eta_{1t}\) moves \(x_t\), which moves \(y_t\), so \(y_t\) and \(\eta_{1t}\) move together: \(\text{Cov}(y_t, \eta_{1t}) \neq 0\). The key condition is violated; OLS then credits the regressor with movement that in reality belongs to the error, and returns a biased and inconsistent estimate. This is the classic simultaneity bias, and it is exactly why the traditional simultaneous-equation models had to rely on instrumental variables and exclusion restrictions to break the link between regressors and errors: the credibility of those restrictions is what Sims contested. The moral is clear: written explicitly into the equations, simultaneity makes the model impossible to estimate directly.

The way out:


Premultiplying ?eq-svar-B by \(B^{-1}\) isolates \(Z_t\) on the left. Indeed \(B^{-1} B = I\) and on the left-hand side what is left is \(Z_t\), while every term on the right is equal to the previous one multiplied by \(B^{-1}\):
\[B^{-1} B\, Z_t = B^{-1}\Gamma\, Z_{t-1} + B^{-1}\eta_t
\;\;\Longrightarrow\;\;
Z_t = \underbrace{B^{-1}\Gamma}_{\Phi}\, Z_{t-1} + \underbrace{B^{-1}\eta_t}_{w_t}\] {#eq-svar-reduced}
The contemporaneous terms are gone: only lagged variables remain on the right-hand side and this is what makes OLS valid here. The regressors are all dated \(t-1\), while the error \(w_t\) is dated \(t\). A value already determined in the previous period cannot move together with a shock that arrives only in the current one, so regressors and error are necessarily uncorrelated and the condition of step 2 is satisfied. Each equation can therefore be estimated separately by OLS. This is the reduced-form VAR of ?eq-var1-matrix-const, and this is why estimation is simple.

Where the simultaneity goes:


It does not disappear. Two properties of the reduced-form residuals must be kept apart, and they point in opposite directions.
Within each equation the residuals must be white noise: serially uncorrelated with its own past, as a well-specified model requires. Residual autocorrelation within equation signals misspecification, typically too few lags, and would call for a better specification.
Across equations, by contrast, the two residuals are correlated at the same date \(t\), because each one blends the same underlying structural shocks (as shown mathematically below). This contemporaneous cross-equation correlation is the footprint left by simultaneity: no longer explicitly visible on the equations, but hided in the correlation between residuals across equation, which leads to a variance covariance matrix in which the term outside the diagonal are not zero. Residual correlation across equation call for identification.
This is precisely why the reduced form alone cannot answer structural questions. The goal of impulse-response analysis is to trace the effect of a single structural shock in isolation, \(\eta_{1t}\) on its own. But the observed residuals never move one at a time: since \(w_{1t}\) and \(w_{2t}\) are correlated, a movement in the first equation arrives together with a movement in the second, and what one observes is the combined effect of both, not the bare effect of a single shock. Recovering the separate structural shocks from these blended residuals is the task of identification, taken up in Chapter 5.
Mathematical and numerical example:

The mechanism in the two-variable VAR(1):


The blending is visible by writing \(w_t = B^{-1}\eta_t\) in extended form, step by step.
The inverse of \(B\) is
\[B^{-1} = \frac{1}{1 - \beta_{12}\beta_{21}} \begin{bmatrix} 1 & \beta_{12} \\ \beta_{21} & 1 \end{bmatrix},\]
so the residual vector, defined as \(w_t = B^{-1}\eta_t\), expands as
\[\begin{bmatrix} w_{1t} \\ w_{2t} \end{bmatrix}
= \frac{1}{1 - \beta_{12}\beta_{21}}
\begin{bmatrix} 1 & \beta_{12} \\ \beta_{21} & 1 \end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix} \eta_{1t} \\ \eta_{2t} \end{bmatrix}
= \frac{1}{1 - \beta_{12}\beta_{21}}
\begin{bmatrix} \eta_{1t} + \beta_{12}\,\eta_{2t} \\ \beta_{21}\,\eta_{1t} + \eta_{2t} \end{bmatrix}.\]
Row by row, each reduced-form residual is a linear combination of both structural shocks:
\[\begin{aligned}
w_{1t} &= \frac{1}{1 - \beta_{12}\beta_{21}}\left(\eta_{1t} + \beta_{12}\,\eta_{2t}\right)\\[4pt]
w_{2t} &= \frac{1}{1 - \beta_{12}\beta_{21}}\left(\beta_{21}\,\eta_{1t} + \eta_{2t}\right)
\end{aligned}\] {#eq-w-blend}

A numerical case:


Let \(\beta_{12} = \beta_{21} = 0.5\), so that \(1 - \beta_{12}\beta_{21} = 0.75\) and
\[w_{1t} = \tfrac{4}{3}\,\eta_{1t} + \tfrac{2}{3}\,\eta_{2t}, \qquad
w_{2t} = \tfrac{2}{3}\,\eta_{1t} + \tfrac{4}{3}\,\eta_{2t}.\]
Suppose the structural shocks are uncorrelated and have unit variance, the most innocent case possible. Then
\[\operatorname{Cov}(w_{1t}, w_{2t}) = \tfrac{4}{3}\cdot\tfrac{2}{3} + \tfrac{2}{3}\cdot\tfrac{4}{3} = \tfrac{16}{9},
\qquad
\operatorname{Var}(w_{1t}) = \operatorname{Var}(w_{2t}) = \tfrac{16}{9} + \tfrac{4}{9} = \tfrac{20}{9},\]
giving a correlation of \(\tfrac{16}{9} \big/ \tfrac{20}{9} = \tfrac{16}{20} = 0.80\). Two perfectly independent structural shocks produce observed residuals correlated at eighty percent: the correlation reflects no link between the primitive causes, only the simultaneity encoded in \(B\).
It does not disappear. Writing \(w_t = B^{-1}\eta_t\) out for the two-variable VAR(1) shows why. With
\[B^{-1} = \frac{1}{1 - \beta_{12}\beta_{21}} \begin{bmatrix} 1 & \beta_{12} \\ \beta_{21} & 1 \end{bmatrix},\]
each reduced-form residual is a linear combination of both structural shocks:
\[\begin{aligned}
w_{1t} &= \frac{1}{1 - \beta_{12}\beta_{21}}\left(\eta_{1t} + \beta_{12}\,\eta_{2t}\right)\\[4pt]
w_{2t} &= \frac{1}{1 - \beta_{12}\beta_{21}}\left(\beta_{21}\,\eta_{1t} + \eta_{2t}\right)
\end{aligned}\] {#eq-w-blend}
Two properties must be kept apart. Within each equation the residual is white noise: serially uncorrelated with its own past, as a well-specified model requires. Residual autocorrelation would instead signal misspecification, typically too few lags, and would call for a different specification rather than for identification. Across equations, however, the two residuals are correlated at the same date \(t\), because they share the same underlying shocks \(\eta_{1t}\) and \(\eta_{2t}\). This contemporaneous cross-equation correlation is the footprint left by simultaneity, now stored in the residual covariance matrix rather than on the right-hand side.
A numerical case makes the size of the effect concrete. Let \(\beta_{12} = \beta_{21} = 0.5\), so that \(1 - \beta_{12}\beta_{21} = 0.75\) and
\[w_{1t} = \tfrac{4}{3}\,\eta_{1t} + \tfrac{2}{3}\,\eta_{2t}, \qquad
w_{2t} = \tfrac{2}{3}\,\eta_{1t} + \tfrac{4}{3}\,\eta_{2t}.\]
Suppose the structural shocks are uncorrelated and have unit variance, the most innocent case possible. Then
\[\operatorname{Cov}(w_{1t}, w_{2t}) = \tfrac{4}{3}\cdot\tfrac{2}{3} + \tfrac{2}{3}\cdot\tfrac{4}{3} = \tfrac{16}{9},
\qquad
\operatorname{Var}(w_{1t}) = \operatorname{Var}(w_{2t}) = \tfrac{16}{9} + \tfrac{4}{9} = \tfrac{20}{9},\]
giving a correlation of \(\tfrac{16}{9} \big/ \tfrac{20}{9} = \tfrac{16}{20} = 0.80\).
So two perfectly independent structural shocks produce observed residuals correlated at eighty percent, magnitude apart, it is pretty intuitive since both are linear combination of the same variable.
This is precisely why the reduced form alone cannot answer structural questions. The goal of impulse-response analysis is to trace the effect of a single structural shock in isolation, \(\eta_{1t}\) on its own. But the observed residuals never move one at a time: because \(w_{1t}\) and \(w_{2t}\) are correlated, a movement attributed to the first equation arrives together with a movement in the second, and the two effects cannot be separated by inspection. Hitting the system with \(\eta_{1t}\) alone is not something the reduced form lets one observe directly. Recovering the separate structural shocks from these blended residuals is the task of identification, taken up in Chapter 5.
In short, simultaneity is never removed, only moved: the structural shocks enter uncorrelated, the matrix \(B^{-1}\) blends them, and what remains observable are reduced-form residuals correlated across equations. The animation below traces this passage from left to right, from the separate structural shocks \(\eta\), through the mixing step \(B^{-1}\), to the blended residuals \(w\) whose cross-equation correlation is exactly what identification, in Chapter 5, will have to undo.

